  <MedicalNote />


  <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/beginner-workout.svg" alt="Illustration of beginner-friendly exercises with proper form cues." />

  <h2>Why All-In-One Exercises Work for Beginners</h2>
  <p>Traditional workout routines often overwhelm beginners with complex programming and isolated movements. All-in-one exercises simplify training by combining multiple muscle groups into single, fluid motions that build coordination, strength, and confidence simultaneously.</p>

  <div class="not-prose">
    <div class="card my-6 bg-brand/5 p-5">
      <strong class="text-ink">Beginner Safety Tip:</strong>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body">Always prioritize form over intensity. It is better to do fewer perfect reps than many sloppy ones.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Top 5 Beginner All-In-One Exercises</h2>

  <h3>1. Incline Push-Ups</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Chest, shoulders, triceps, core, quads</p>
  <p><strong>How to modify:</strong> Use a wall, sturdy table, or countertop. The higher the surface, the easier the exercise.</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner tip:</strong> Start with wall push-ups if you cannot do 8 incline push-ups yet.</p>

  <h3>2. Chair-Assisted Squats</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Quads, glutes, hamstrings, calves, core</p>
  <p><strong>How to modify:</strong> Hold onto a sturdy chair for balance, or use the chair to tap your butt for depth feedback.</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner tip:</strong> Sit back slowly as if lowering into a chair, then stand through your heels.</p>

  <h3>3. Glute Bridges</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Glutes, hamstrings, core, lower back</p>
  <p><strong>How to modify:</strong> Keep feet flat on floor, hands by sides for support.</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner tip:</strong> Focus on squeezing your glutes at the top, hold for 2 seconds.</p>

  <h3>4. Modified Plank (Knee Plank)</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Core, shoulders, chest, glutes</p>
  <p><strong>How to modify:</strong> Drop to knees instead of full plank, reduce hold time.</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner tip:</strong> Keep body in straight line from shoulders to knees.</p>

  <h3>5. Step-Back Lunges</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Quads, glutes, hamstrings, calves</p>
  <p><strong>How to modify:</strong> Use a wall for balance, reduce range of motion.</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner tip:</strong> Take a shorter step and focus on the lowering phase.</p>

